{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"churlish-fruited-dassie","creation_channel":"cli","description":"Standardize the CLI write-command argument parsing. Today the CLI parses args with a HAND-ROLLED manual loop (see src/rebar/_commands/claim.py _parse_force/_parse_assignee, and the transition path) carrying bespoke, undocumented-in-schema semantics: a bare --force yields a default reason \"(no reason given)\"; --assignee ABSENT is a None sentinel (=> ticket.default_assignee fallback) while explicit --assignee \"\" CLEARS the assignee. This is tech-debt: inconsistent flags and --help that can silently drift from behavior.\n\nGOAL: move the CLI adapter toward a standard framework (argparse/Click/Typer, as maintained OSS CLIs do) for consistency + accurate generated --help, WITHOUT changing behavior. This is an ADAPTER REFACTOR, orthogonal to the parity oracle; the oracle then simply also validates the standardized CLI (no blending of patterns).\n\nCONTRACT PRESERVATION (must not regress): flag spellings, the exit-code contract (10 = ConcurrencyError), the --force default-reason semantics, and the --assignee None-vs-empty distinction must be preserved exactly (or intentionally changed with a tombstone + doc update).\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n- Write-command parsing goes through the standard framework; --help is generated and accurate.\n- The behavioral parity oracle passes for the CLI adapter with identical accept/reject/effect (incl. --force default reason, --assignee sentinel).\n- Exit codes unchanged (10 concurrency); existing CLI interface tests pass.\n- make lint + typecheck green; module-size + complexity ratchets respected.","id":"fd48-7a80-33e9-4400","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Standardize CLI write-command arg parsing (retire hand-rolled loop for a std framework)"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787080925392033001,"uuid":"74c4c275-48ad-4fd4-a706-f68cd21394fc"}